###Pure
// ============================================================
// trade-lifecycle (L2) -- what happens to a trade after it is captured
// ============================================================
// Depends on trade-capture (the trade being changed) and event-core (the corporate
// action on the underlying that forces one of those changes). Nothing else.
//
// The argument this project makes is that a post-trade change is not an UPDATE. A trade
// is amended, novated, partially terminated, compressed or unwound, and each of those
// arrives as a NEW TLC_AMENDMENT row that names the row it replaces in SUPERSEDES_ID.
// Nothing is overwritten, so the chain of rows IS the audit trail: replay it forwards and
// you have every state the trade was ever in, and "the current version" is not a flag or a
// max(version) but the one row nothing supersedes.
//
// That is why the {target} SELF-JOIN Tlc_SupersededBy is the construct at the centre of
// this project, and why trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentChain has TlcAmendment on BOTH ends
// -- both of its mapping ends name [tlcAmendment] twice, and only the direction of travel
// over the join tells `supersedes` from `supersededBy`.
//
// The second construct is the Enum with an EnumerationMapping. TlcAmendmentType and its
// six siblings are Pure enums; the FpML-ish and back-office code strings that the physical
// tables actually hold ('NOVA', 'PTRM', 'CMPR') are confined to the EnumerationMappings in
// mapping.pure and never appear on a class.
//
// The cross-dependency is TlcCorporateActionImpact. A dividend, a split or a merger on the
// underlying changes the terms of a trade struck on it -- and the way that change is
// recorded here is the same way every other change is recorded: it raises an amendment,
// which supersedes the last one. So event_core::EvtCorporateEvent, trade_capture::Trade
// and this project's own chain all meet on one class.
//
// Prefixes: tables TLC_, joins Tlc_, filters Tlc, set ids tlc.

// ---- Enums ----

// FOR: which kind of post-trade change one amendment row is. This is the single fact that
// says which of the detail tables below has a row for it -- a NOVATION has a TLC_NOVATION
// row, a COMPRESSION has a TLC_COMPRESSION_MEMBER row -- so it is the discriminator a
// reader of the chain dispatches on.
Enum trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentType
{
   ECONOMIC_AMENDMENT,
   NON_ECONOMIC_AMENDMENT,
   NOVATION,
   PARTIAL_TERMINATION,
   FULL_TERMINATION,
   COMPRESSION,
   UNWIND,
   EXERCISE,
   EXPIRY,
   RATE_RESET,
   CORPORATE_ACTION,
   RESTRUCTURING,
   CORRECTION
}

// FOR: how far through its own approval an amendment has got. SUPERSEDED is the one that
// is not a workflow state at all: it is what an APPLIED amendment becomes when a later
// amendment names it, and it is set by the arrival of the next row rather than by anyone
// acting on this one.
Enum trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentStatus
{
   DRAFT,
   PENDING_APPROVAL,
   APPROVED,
   APPLIED,
   SUPERSEDED,
   REJECTED,
   WITHDRAWN
}

// FOR: whether a party has agreed to a novation. DEEMED is the interesting one -- consent
// deemed given because the notice period expired without a refusal, which is legally a yes
// and operationally a silence, and the two must not be recorded as the same thing.
Enum trade_lifecycle::TlcConsentStatus
{
   REQUESTED,
   GRANTED,
   REFUSED,
   DEEMED,
   LAPSED
}

// FOR: why a trade ended early. This drives the money: a MUTUAL_AGREEMENT unwind is priced
// at mid and a DEFAULT close-out is priced at the non-defaulting party's replacement cost,
// which are different numbers for the same trade on the same day.
Enum trade_lifecycle::TlcTerminationReason
{
   MUTUAL_AGREEMENT,
   COUNTERPARTY_DEFAULT,
   CREDIT_EVENT,
   REGULATORY_REQUIREMENT,
   PORTFOLIO_COMPRESSION,
   OPTIONAL_BREAK,
   ERROR_CORRECTION
}

// FOR: how a compression cycle was run. Whether the netting was bilateral or multilateral
// decides who had to consent before the trades could be torn up, so it is a legal fact and
// not merely a description of an algorithm.
Enum trade_lifecycle::TlcCompressionMethod
{
   BILATERAL,
   MULTILATERAL,
   PORTFOLIO_RECONCILIATION,
   RISK_CONSTRAINED,
   COUPON_BLENDING
}

// FOR: how the counterparty was told. The channel is the evidence: a SWIFT message has a
// delivery receipt and an EMAIL does not, which is what an operations team argues about
// when a novation is disputed.
Enum trade_lifecycle::TlcNotificationChannel
{
   SWIFT,
   FPML,
   EMAIL,
   CLIENT_PORTAL,
   FAX,
   PHONE
}

// FOR: where the human workflow behind an amendment has got to, as distinct from the
// amendment's own status. BLOCKED is not REJECTED: a blocked step is waiting on someone
// outside the firm, and the amendment is still going to happen.
Enum trade_lifecycle::TlcWorkflowState
{
   OPEN,
   IN_PROGRESS,
   BLOCKED,
   COMPLETED,
   CANCELLED
}

// ---- Classes ----

// FOR: the one row that IS a lifecycle change. Every kind of change -- an economic amend,
// a novation, a compression tear-up -- is one of these, and the detail classes below only
// carry the terms specific to their kind. Because a change never overwrites, this class is
// on both ends of TlcAmendmentChain: `supersedesId` points at the row this one replaces,
// and the row nothing points at is the trade as it stands today.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendment
[
   versionIsPositive: ($this.versionNumber > 0),
   // A superseding row must be at least as recent as the one it supersedes; a chain that
   // goes backwards in time is a keying error, not a correction.
   appliedNotBeforeRequested:
      (($this.appliedAt->isEmpty()) || ($this.appliedAt->toOne() >= $this.requestedAt)),
   // The first row in a chain is version 1 and supersedes nothing. Anything later must name
   // what it replaces, or the audit trail has a hole in it.
   chainRootIsVersionOne:
      (($this.supersedesId->isNotEmpty()) || ($this.versionNumber == 1))
]
{
   amendmentId: String[1];
   // The AMENDMENT_ID of the row this one replaces. Empty exactly once per trade, on the
   // first change ever made to it.
   supersedesId: String[0..1];
   amendmentType: trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentType[1];
   status: trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentStatus[1];
   versionNumber: Integer[1];
   effectiveDate: StrictDate[1];
   requestedAt: DateTime[1];
   appliedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   requestedBy: String[1];
   approvedBy: String[0..1];
   isEconomic: Boolean[1];
   isClientInitiated: Boolean[1];
   withdrawnOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   externalReference: String[0..1];
   narrative: String[0..1];

   // The head of the chain: nothing supersedes it, so this is the trade as it stands.
   // Deliberately expressed over the association and not over a status flag -- a flag can
   // be stale, an absent superseding row cannot.
   isCurrentVersion() { $this.supersededBy->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];

   // The root of the chain: the first thing that ever happened to this trade.
   isChainRoot() { $this.supersedesId->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];

   // Applied but not yet replaced -- the state a downstream reader wants.
   isLive() { ($this.withdrawnOn->isEmpty()) && ($this.supersededBy->isEmpty()) } : Boolean[1];
}

// FOR: what actually changed, field by field. The amendment says a change happened; this
// says the notional went from 10,000,000 to 7,500,000. Kept as rows rather than columns
// because one amendment may move four fields and the next one may move a field nobody
// anticipated.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentDetail
{
   detailId: String[1];
   fieldName: String[1];
   oldValue: String[0..1];
   newValue: String[0..1];
   valueType: String[1];
   isEconomicField: Boolean[1];
   sequenceNumber: Integer[1];
}

// FOR: the controlled vocabulary of WHY. Held as its own class rather than a free-text
// column on the amendment because the reason drives reporting obligations -- a change made
// for a trade error is reportable and a change made for a client request is not.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentReason
{
   reasonId: String[1];
   reasonCode: String[1];
   description: String[1];
   requiresApproval: Boolean[1];
   isReportable: Boolean[1];
   regulatoryCategory: String[0..1];
}

// FOR: proof that a human agreed before the change was applied. Separate from the
// amendment's `approvedBy` because an amendment can need two approvals, and because the
// approval survives even if the amendment is later withdrawn.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentApproval
{
   approvalId: String[1];
   approverName: String[1];
   approverRole: String[1];
   approvedAt: DateTime[1];
   approvalLevel: Integer[1];
   isOverride: Boolean[1];
   comment: String[0..1];
}

// FOR: the transfer of one side of a trade to a different counterparty. The economics do
// not change and the counterparty does, which is why a novation is an amendment and not a
// termination plus a new trade -- the chain has to stay unbroken across it.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcNovation
[
   novatedNotionalIsPositive: ($this.novatedNotional > 0.0),
   partiesDiffer: ($this.transferorReference != $this.transfereeReference)
]
{
   novationId: String[1];
   transferorReference: String[1];
   transfereeReference: String[1];
   remainingPartyReference: String[1];
   novationDate: StrictDate[1];
   novatedNotional: Float[1];
   currency: String[1];
   feeAmount: Float[0..1];
   isFullNovation: Boolean[1];
   agreementReference: String[0..1];
}

// FOR: one party's answer to a novation request. A novation needs the remaining party's
// consent as well as the transferee's, and either can refuse, so consent is a row per
// party and not a flag on the novation.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcNovationConsent
{
   consentId: String[1];
   partyReference: String[1];
   partyRole: String[1];
   status: trade_lifecycle::TlcConsentStatus[1];
   requestedAt: DateTime[1];
   respondedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   noticeDeadline: StrictDate[0..1];
   refusalReason: String[0..1];
}

// FOR: a reduction in notional that leaves the trade alive. The residual notional is
// stored as well as the reduction because the two are asserted independently by the two
// parties, and a partial termination that disagrees on the residual is a break.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcPartialTermination
[
   reductionIsPositive: ($this.reducedNotional > 0.0),
   residualIsNonNegative: ($this.residualNotional >= 0.0)
]
{
   partialTerminationId: String[1];
   terminationDate: StrictDate[1];
   originalNotional: Float[1];
   reducedNotional: Float[1];
   residualNotional: Float[1];
   currency: String[1];
   reason: trade_lifecycle::TlcTerminationReason[1];
   settlementAmount: Float[0..1];

   // The share of the trade taken off. `/` widens to Float even on two Integers, so this
   // must be declared Float regardless of what its operands are.
   reductionRatio() { $this.reducedNotional / $this.originalNotional } : Float[1];
}

// FOR: the end of the trade. Distinguished from a cancellation in trade-capture: a
// cancellation says the trade never should have existed, a termination says it existed and
// has now stopped, and only the second one leaves a settlement amount behind.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcTermination
{
   terminationId: String[1];
   terminationDate: StrictDate[1];
   effectiveDate: StrictDate[1];
   reason: trade_lifecycle::TlcTerminationReason[1];
   terminationAmount: Float[0..1];
   currency: String[0..1];
   isEarlyTermination: Boolean[1];
   calculationAgent: String[0..1];
   noticeReference: String[0..1];
}

// FOR: one run of a compression exercise, as an event in its own right. Several trades are
// torn up and at most one replacement is booked; the cycle is what ties those otherwise
// unrelated amendments together, which no single trade's chain can express.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcCompressionCycle
[
   tradesInIsPositive: ($this.tradeCountBefore > 0)
]
{
   cycleId: String[1];
   cycleReference: String[1];
   runDate: StrictDate[1];
   method: trade_lifecycle::TlcCompressionMethod[1];
   serviceProvider: String[0..1];
   tradeCountBefore: Integer[1];
   tradeCountAfter: Integer[1];
   notionalBefore: Float[1];
   notionalAfter: Float[1];
   currency: String[1];

   // How much gross notional the cycle removed. Both operands are Float, so no widening
   // question arises here.
   notionalRemoved() { $this.notionalBefore - $this.notionalAfter } : Float[1];

   // The compression ratio the service provider reports. Float because `/` widens.
   compressionRatio() { $this.notionalAfter / $this.notionalBefore } : Float[1];
}

// FOR: one trade's participation in one compression cycle. Carries the per-trade outcome,
// because a cycle can terminate one trade, reduce a second and leave a third untouched
// after including it in the netting.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcCompressionMember
{
   memberId: String[1];
   notionalBefore: Float[1];
   notionalAfter: Float[1];
   outcome: String[1];
   isReplacementTrade: Boolean[1];
   riskDelta: Float[0..1];
}

// FOR: an early exit priced at market rather than at the contract's own terms. An unwind
// and a termination end the same trade; the unwind is the one where a price had to be
// agreed, so it carries the mid, the spread and who calculated them.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcUnwind
{
   unwindId: String[1];
   unwindDate: StrictDate[1];
   unwindPrice: Float[1];
   midPrice: Float[0..1];
   spreadPaid: Float[0..1];
   cashSettlementAmount: Float[1];
   currency: String[1];
   priceSource: String[0..1];
   isNegotiated: Boolean[1];
}

// FOR: the holder taking up an optional right. Modelled here rather than in trade-capture
// because an exercise amends the trade it is on -- an American option exercised early
// stops accruing, which is a change to the trade's terms and belongs in the chain.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcExercise
[
   exercisedQuantityIsPositive: ($this.exercisedQuantity > 0.0)
]
{
   exerciseId: String[1];
   exerciseDate: StrictDate[1];
   exerciseStyle: String[1];
   exercisedQuantity: Float[1];
   remainingQuantity: Float[1];
   strikePrice: Float[0..1];
   settlementMethod: String[1];
   isAutomatic: Boolean[1];
}

// FOR: the trade reaching its own end date without anyone doing anything. Worth a row
// because "nothing happened" is a fact a reconciliation needs asserted: an expiry that was
// never booked and an expiry that was booked look identical on the trade itself.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcExpiry
{
   expiryId: String[1];
   expiryDate: StrictDate[1];
   expiredInTheMoney: Boolean[1];
   finalReferencePrice: Float[0..1];
   payoutAmount: Float[0..1];
   currency: String[0..1];
   wasAutoExercised: Boolean[1];
}

// FOR: a floating rate fixing being observed and applied. It amends the trade because the
// coupon that will be paid is not knowable until it happens, and the value it fixed at is
// evidence -- a rate republished later does not retroactively change what was paid.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcRateReset
{
   resetId: String[1];
   resetDate: StrictDate[1];
   effectiveDate: StrictDate[1];
   rateIndexCode: String[1];
   observedRate: Float[1];
   spread: Float[0..1];
   allInRate: Float[1];
   fixingSource: String[0..1];
   isEstimated: Boolean[1];
}

// FOR: the money a lifecycle event creates or destroys. A termination fee, an unwind
// settlement and a novation fee are all cash that exists only because of an amendment, so
// the cashflow hangs off the amendment rather than off the trade.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcLifecycleCashflow
{
   cashflowId: String[1];
   cashflowType: String[1];
   amount: Float[1];
   currency: String[1];
   valueDate: StrictDate[1];
   payerReference: String[1];
   receiverReference: String[1];
   isSettled: Boolean[1];
   settledOn: StrictDate[0..1];
}

// FOR: the cross-dependency, and the reason this project depends on event-core at all. A
// dividend, a split or a merger on the underlying changes the terms of every trade struck
// on it -- the strike is adjusted, the quantity is scaled -- and that change enters the
// trade's history as an ordinary amendment. One row here ties one corporate action to one
// trade to the amendment it caused.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcCorporateActionImpact
{
   impactId: String[1];
   adjustmentFactor: Float[1];
   originalQuantity: Float[1];
   adjustedQuantity: Float[1];
   originalStrike: Float[0..1];
   adjustedStrike: Float[0..1];
   cashCompensation: Float[0..1];
   currency: String[0..1];
   adjustmentMethod: String[1];
   isMandatory: Boolean[1];
   appliedOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   // Whether the action actually moved the trade. A record-date-only event can produce an
   // impact row with a factor of one, which is not the same fact as no row at all.
   isMaterial() { $this.adjustmentFactor != 1.0 } : Boolean[1];
}

// FOR: telling the counterparty. The notice, not the amendment, is what starts a consent
// clock running, so its channel and timestamp are the operative facts in any later dispute
// about whether a novation was validly effected.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcEventNotification
{
   notificationId: String[1];
   channel: trade_lifecycle::TlcNotificationChannel[1];
   recipientReference: String[1];
   sentAt: DateTime[1];
   acknowledgedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   messageReference: String[0..1];
   deliveryStatus: String[1];
   retryCount: Integer[1];
}

// FOR: the human process behind an amendment, as an object. An amendment can sit for three
// days waiting on a client; the workflow is where that waiting is visible, and it is
// separate from the amendment's status because the amendment is one state and the process
// is several.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcLifecycleWorkflow
{
   workflowId: String[1];
   workflowType: String[1];
   state: trade_lifecycle::TlcWorkflowState[1];
   openedAt: DateTime[1];
   completedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   assignedTeam: String[0..1];
   slaHours: Integer[0..1];
   isBreached: Boolean[1];
}

// FOR: one thing that had to be done. Kept as rows so the order and the gaps are visible;
// the step that was skipped is as interesting to a control function as the ones that were
// completed.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcWorkflowStep
{
   stepId: String[1];
   stepName: String[1];
   sequenceNumber: Integer[1];
   state: trade_lifecycle::TlcWorkflowState[1];
   startedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   completedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   performedBy: String[0..1];
   isMandatory: Boolean[1];
}

// FOR: the immutable record of who touched what. The amendment chain is already an audit
// trail of the trade's ECONOMICS; this is the audit trail of the SYSTEM -- who opened the
// screen, what they typed, which of it was rejected before an amendment ever existed.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcAuditEntry
{
   auditId: String[1];
   action: String[1];
   performedBy: String[1];
   performedAt: DateTime[1];
   fieldTouched: String[0..1];
   previousValue: String[0..1];
   resultingValue: String[0..1];
   sourceIpAddress: String[0..1];
}

// FOR: where the lifecycle event came from. A change asserted by a middleware feed and the
// same change typed by a person carry different weight when two systems disagree, and the
// source is the only place that difference is recorded.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcEventSource
{
   sourceId: String[1];
   sourceCode: String[1];
   sourceName: String[1];
   isAutomated: Boolean[1];
   isTrusted: Boolean[1];
   contactReference: String[0..1];
}

// FOR: the paper. A novation has an executed novation agreement behind it and a
// termination has a notice; the document reference is what an auditor asks for, and it is
// not derivable from anything else in the chain.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcSupportingDocument
{
   documentId: String[1];
   documentType: String[1];
   documentReference: String[1];
   receivedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   isExecuted: Boolean[1];
   storageLocation: String[0..1];
}

// FOR: a credit event forcing a trade to be restructured rather than terminated. It is its
// own class because the successor obligation and the recovery are terms that no other kind
// of amendment carries, and inventing nullable columns on the amendment for them would
// bury the distinction.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcRestructuring
{
   restructuringId: String[1];
   restructuringDate: StrictDate[1];
   creditEventType: String[1];
   successorReference: String[0..1];
   recoveryRate: Float[0..1];
   auctionReference: String[0..1];
   isSuccessorDetermined: Boolean[1];
}

// FOR: moving an allocation between end clients after the trade has been allocated. It is
// a lifecycle event and not a re-allocation in trade-capture because the original
// allocation was reported, and unreporting it is itself a reportable act.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcAllocationAdjustment
[
   adjustedQuantityIsPositive: ($this.adjustedQuantity > 0.0)
]
{
   adjustmentId: String[1];
   originalAccountReference: String[1];
   newAccountReference: String[1];
   adjustedQuantity: Float[1];
   adjustmentDate: StrictDate[1];
   isReportable: Boolean[1];
   reportedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
}

// FOR: fixing something that was simply wrong. A correction is the one amendment type that
// asserts the previous row never described reality -- which is why it still supersedes
// rather than deletes, and why it carries the original booking timestamp it is correcting.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcCorrection
{
   correctionId: String[1];
   correctedAt: DateTime[1];
   originalBookedAt: DateTime[1];
   correctionCategory: String[1];
   wasReported: Boolean[1];
   regulatoryResubmissionRef: String[0..1];
   detectedBy: String[0..1];
}

// FOR: the money charged for ending early. It is not the same as the unwind settlement:
// the settlement is the value of the remaining trade, the break fee is the penalty for not
// letting it run, and a contract can specify one without the other.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcBreakFee
[
   feeIsNonNegative: ($this.feeAmount >= 0.0)
]
{
   breakFeeId: String[1];
   feeAmount: Float[1];
   currency: String[1];
   basisPoints: Float[0..1];
   calculationBasis: String[1];
   isWaived: Boolean[1];
   waivedBy: String[0..1];
}

// FOR: the trade as it read after one amendment was applied. The chain records the
// CHANGES; this records the RESULT, so a query for "what did this trade look like on the
// 14th" does not have to replay every amendment from the beginning to find out.
Class trade_lifecycle::TlcVersionSnapshot
[
   snapshotVersionIsPositive: ($this.versionNumber > 0)
]
{
   snapshotId: String[1];
   versionNumber: Integer[1];
   asOfDate: StrictDate[1];
   quantity: Float[1];
   price: Float[1];
   notional: Float[1];
   currency: String[1];
   counterpartyReference: String[0..1];
   maturityDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   isCurrent: Boolean[1];
}

// ---- Associations ----

// THE self-join association. TlcAmendment is on BOTH ends, so both of its mapping ends
// name [tlcAmendment, tlcAmendment] -- the same set id twice -- and only the direction of
// travel over Tlc_SupersededBy tells the two apart. `supersedes` reads SUPERSEDES_ID and
// walks BACK one link; `supersededBy` walks FORWARD to whoever named this row.
Association trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentChain
{
   supersedes: trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendment[0..1];
   supersededBy: trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendment[0..1];
}

// ---- Cross-project associations ----
// End names on trade_capture::Trade are all `lifecycle`-qualified, because that class
// already carries `amendments`, `versions`, `cancellations` and `allocations` from
// trade-capture and a second `amendments` would collide.

Association trade_lifecycle::TlcTradeAmendments
{
   amendedTrade: trade_capture::Trade[0..1];
   lifecycleAmendments: trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendment[*];
}

Association trade_lifecycle::TlcTradeNovations
{
   novatedTrade: trade_capture::Trade[0..1];
   lifecycleNovations: trade_lifecycle::TlcNovation[*];
}

Association trade_lifecycle::TlcTradeSnapshots
{
   snapshotTrade: trade_capture::Trade[0..1];
   lifecycleSnapshots: trade_lifecycle::TlcVersionSnapshot[*];
}

Association trade_lifecycle::TlcTradeCompressionMembers
{
   compressedTrade: trade_capture::Trade[0..1];
   lifecycleCompressionMembers: trade_lifecycle::TlcCompressionMember[*];
}

Association trade_lifecycle::TlcTradeCorporateActionImpacts
{
   impactedTrade: trade_capture::Trade[0..1];
   lifecycleCorporateActionImpacts: trade_lifecycle::TlcCorporateActionImpact[*];
}

// The other half of the cross-dependency: the corporate action itself. `tradeImpacts` is
// hung on event_core::EvtCorporateEvent, which already carries `instrument` from
// event-core's own cross-project association.
Association trade_lifecycle::TlcImpactCorporateAction
{
   corporateAction: event_core::EvtCorporateEvent[0..1];
   tradeImpacts: trade_lifecycle::TlcCorporateActionImpact[*];
}

// ---- Associations inside trade-lifecycle ----

Association trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentDetails
{
   details: trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentDetail[*];
   detailAmendment: trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendment[0..1];
}

Association trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentReasonLink
{
   reason: trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentReason[0..1];
   reasonAmendments: trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendment[*];
}

Association trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentApprovals
{
   approvals: trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentApproval[*];
   approvalAmendment: trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendment[0..1];
}

Association trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendmentNovation
{
   novations: trade_lifecycle::TlcNovation[*];
   novationAmendment: trade_lifecycle::TlcAmendment[0..1];
}

